/**
\page properties Node Properties in yoyo

Nodes make up the Yoyo data-model. They are organized in a tree structure (thus the name). Each node
has a set of properties, that define its behavior and/or the behavior of its neighboring nodes.

There are some properties all nodes have in common, like the name (identifier) or the read-only
property type. But mostly they are defined for the individual node type.

All properties will be displayed and can be modified by the user with the \ref modules_gui_property,
though some properties may be hidden, depending on their type and value.

All non read-only properties are (to be) persisted and set, when the node is deserialized. (Some
persistency implementations may choose not to persist properties with default values.)

\section properties_concept Concept

Yoyo uses the Qt property system to define a property for a node class. A read-writable property is
defined like this:

\code{.cpp}
Q_PROPERTY(type propertyName READ propertyGetter WRITE propertySetter NOTIFY changedSignal)
\endcode

For read-only properties, only the READ attribute is necessary, which requires you to specify the
getter for the property, which in Qt usually has the name of the property with a signature like
this:

\code{.cpp}
auto propertyName() const -> type;
\endcode

For writable properties, in addition to the READ attribute also the WRITE attribute is necessary,
specifying the setter for the property. In Qt this is usually of this signature:

\code{.cpp}
auto setPropertyName(type value) void;
\endcode

In previous Qt versions, this method needed to be declared as Q_SLOT. Fortunately, this is no more
necessary.

Even though not strictly necessary a notifier signal should be specified as well using the NOTIFY
attribute (Exception: constant properties). This allows you to notify other components of Yoyo
(\ref modules_model_connection engine or \ref modules_gui_property), that the value of the property
changed.

\code{.cpp}
Q_SIGNALS:
  void propertyNameChanged(type value);
\endcode

\note In case of signal definition, the use of trailing return types, which is predominant in Yoyo,
    has been omitted, to prevent hickups from the Qt meta object compiler.

In each of the code snipplets, "type" has to be exchanged with the actual type of the property and
"propertyName" with the respective name.

\warning In a Q_PROPERTY declaration, the type must always contain the complete type, includin
    namespace specifiers independent of the namespace the class is declared in (Example:
    yoyo::types::access_t instead of types::access_t even if declared in yoyo namespace).

\section properties_defined Defined property types

Each property has a type. For Yoyo, the allowed property types are restricted due to several
factors:

- First, the used type needs to be available to the Qt type system
    (https://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qmetatype.html) to be able to be transported via the Qt property system.
- Second, Yoyo has to support the display/edit of the type in the \ref modules_gui_property and it
    has to support the serialization and deserialization of the type in \b all defined io-formats.

This is the list of currently supported property types:

\subsection properties_supported_types_numeric Numeric types
|Type|Description|
|:--|:----------------|
|char| a signed character (presumably 8-bit)|
|short| a signed short integer (presumably 16-bit)|
|int| a signed integer (presumably 32-bit)|
|long| a signed long integer (presumably 64-bit)|
|long long| a signed long long integer (presumably 64-bit)|
|unsigned char| an unsigned character (presumably 8-bit)|
|unsigned short| an unsigned short integer (presumably 16-bit)|
|unsigned int| an unsigned integer (presumably 32-bit)|
|unsigned long| an unsigned long integer (presumably 64-bit)|
|unsigned long long| an unsigned long long integer (presumably 64-bit)|
|int8_t| fixed size signed integer 8-bit |
|int16_t| fixed size signed integer 16-bit |
|int32_t| fixed size signed integer 32-bit |
|int64_t| fixed size signed integer 64-bit |
|uint8_t| fixed size unsigned integer 8-bit |
|uint16_t| fixed size unsigned integer 16-bit |
|uint32_t| fixed size unsigned integer 32-bit |
|uint64_t| fixed size unsigned integer 64-bit |
|float| a floating point value, single precision|
|double| a floating point value, double precision|

\subsection properties_supported_types_enum Enumeration types
|Type|Description|
|:--|:----------------|
| yoyo::types::value_t| The allowed values data nodes may propagate |
| yoyo::types::access_t | Accessability of an object. Can be READ, WRITE, READ_WRITE or NONE|
| yoyo::types::layout_direction_t | Direction of the layout used by gui nodes. Can be FREE, HORIZONTAL or VERTICAL|
| yoyo::types::integer_format_t | The format in which integers shall be displayed by a gui node. This can be BINARY, OCTAL, DECIMAL or HEXAL|

\subsection properties_supported_types_string String types
|Type|Description|
|:--|:----------------|
|QString| a single lined string, may contain line-breaks, but is displayed in a single line|
|yoyo::properties::patterned_string_t| a single lined string, which has to fulfill a regular expression|
|yoyo::properties::text_t| a multi-line string, which will be displayed|

\subsection properties_supported_types_connectivity Connectivity related types
These properties only make sense in nodes of the type gui, protocol or communication. These nodes
types connect to data nodes (not the other way round) and the connection engine will use these
properties and use them when connecting nodes.

|Type|Description|
|:--|:----------------|
|yoyo::properties::connection_t| Defines an in-/out- connection. |
|yoyo::properties::in_connection_t| Defines a read-only connection. |
|yoyo::properties::out_connection_t| Defines a write-only connection. |
|yoyo::properties::connected_string_t| Defines a string to be displayed. If empty on this node, and if the connected node does have a string property of the same name, this property will be used instead.|
|yoyo::properties::connected_boolean_t| Defines a boolean. If DEFINED_BY_CONNECTION, and if the connected node does have a boolean property of the same name, this property will be used.|

\subsection properties_supported_types_yoyo Yoyo functional types
|Type|Description|
|:--|:----------------|
|yoyo::properties::enum_t| Useful for nodes to connect to data nodes which define enumerations as well. Actions in both nodes can be aligned this way |
|yoyo::properties::script_t| A script to execute on some trigger|
|yoyo::properties::limited_int8_t| a signed fixed length integer (8-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::limited_int16_t| a signed fixed length integer (16-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::limited_int32_t| a signed fixed length integer (32-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::limited_int64_t| a signed fixed length integer (64-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::limited_uint8_t| an unsigned fixed length integer (8-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::limited_uint16_t| an unsigned fixed length integer (16-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::limited_uint32_t| an unsigned fixed length integer (32-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::limited_uint64_t| an unsigned fixed length integer (64-bit) with boundaries|
|yoyo::properties::invisible_string_t| A string property, which can be invisible in the Property Widget|
|yoyo::properties::invisible_layout_direction_t| A layout direction property, which can be invisible in the Property Widget|

\subsection properties_supported_types_other Other types
|Type|Description|
|:--|:----------------|
|bool| A boolean number |

\section properties_extend How to extend the list of property types

As already mentioned in the previous section, there are some adjustments needed, when adding new
types to the Yoyo property system.

For the sake of simplicity, let's assume the new type my::own::type_t is to be added to Yoyo.

\subsection properties_extend_header Declare type

First of all, you'll have to declare the type. If you want it to be a privately used type, you can
choose to declare it outside the \ref modules_sdk module. If you want it to be published with the
next version of Yoyo, the file src/sdk/include/yoyo/property_types.h is a good place for it.

\subsection properties_extend_qt Add to Qt meta type system

At the bottom of the file src/sdk/include/yoyo/property_types.h you can see a list already, where
the Yoyo defined types are added to the Qt meta type system, at least part one of two.

\code{.cpp}
Q_DECLARE_METATYPE(my::own::type_t)
\endcode

This must be declared once and only once close to the declaration of the new type itself. It must
use the complete type, including all namespaces. No semicolon is necessary at the end of the
statement.

The next step should be done in a source file, in a function, which is invoked once at startup
(preferably also for tests). The published types do their call in registerAllPropertyTypes, which is
implemented in src/sdk/src/node_base.cpp. Add this line:

\code{.cpp}
qRegisterMetaType<my::own::type_t>();
\endcode

In contrast to the macro call above, you can omit the yoyo namespace specifier, if applicable. Or
the respective one, if you choose to declare it in an other function in an other namespace.

And while you're on it, append the type in the function value_equals in src/sdk/src/node_base.cpp to
allow equality checks (mostly used in tests).

\subsection properties_extend_io Add to Serialization/Deserialization

In the current version, there is only one IO format supported: json, so you only have to modify one
set.

According to the principles of \ref guidelines_coverage, we should first create a test, then add the
production code. For the json format, there is already a pretty convenient test, where the new type
can be easily added. There is two locations:

- tests/unit-tests/io/io_fixture.cpp
- tests/unit-tests/io/json_properties_I_tests.cpp

\subsubsection properties_extend_io_general General

In file io_fixture.cpp, starting from line 141, there are several simplified node classes declared
containing read-writable (but not notifying) properties. Select one node class and add a property
with the new property type here (don't forget the member, if you follow the used pattern).

You can also define a template specialization to the random value generation functions on top for
the new property type. This makes it easy to set some non default values in
IoFixture::setNodeValues; append your code here:
\code{.cpp}
{ std::string { "my::own::type_t" },
  []() -> QVariant {
    return QVariant::fromValue(generateRandomValue<my:own::type_t>(...));
  } },
\endcode

If you decided to create a new test node class, don't forget to install it into the factory in
IoFixture::installNodes().

Comparison also works, if the method IoFixture::expectValuesEqual is not explicitly handling the new
property type, but the output on failure is much easier to read.

This fixture is used by tests/unit-tests/io/json_tests.cpp and will most probably be used by other
formats as well, so adding the handling of the new type here makes it reusable.

\subsubsection properties_extend_io_json Json specific

In the file json_properties_I_tests.cpp, there are two tests verifying, that the json format is able
to handle the all defined property types:

- JsonTest.property: checks, whether the type is added to the serializer/deserializer map. Add a
    line here
    \code{.cpp}
  EXPECT_NE(prop::io.find("my::own::type_t"), prop::io.end());
    \endcode
- JsonTestValues.properties: this is a parametrized test, serializing a value, deserializing it
    again and checking whether the value is still the same. In the instantiation list for this test
    add a line or more like:
    \code{.cpp}
  test_data { QString { "my::own::type_t" }, QVariant::fromValue(my::own::type_t{someInitializer}) },
    \endcode

\subsubsection properties_extend_io Adjust Production Code

When you've read the test modifications closely, you already know, where to add the changes for
production code.

\subsection properties_extend_gui Property Widget

The Property Widget uses for each property an own child widget. To be precise, the actual
implementation of property_widget is less than 100 LOC, and no code is to be added here. The
handling of the individual types is done in classes derived from property_input (see property_input
sub folder).

If you investigate this folder, you will notice, that there is a significant number of classes
(widgets) handling different property types, some widgets handling several related types. This will
be the place, where to add a custom input widget for the new property type. Don't forget to add a
line to property_input_factory.cpp in the same directory:
\code{.cpp}
{ "my::own::type_t", [](auto n, auto p, auto parent) { return std::make_shared<my::own::type_input>(n, p, parent); } },
\endcode

\subsubsection properties_extend_gui_test Tests
But, before changing the production directories, let's again start with the tests.

In directory tests/unit-tests/gui/property/ there are already some tests defined for simplified test
nodes (similar to \ref properties_extend_io_general Tests).

Leaving the file header aside, the test code should contain a testing node definition:
\code{.cpp}
class my_own_node_mock : public mock_node
{
  Q_OBJECT

  Q_PROPERTY(my::own::type_t prop1 READ prop1 WRITE set_prop1 NOTIFY prop1Changed)
public:
  using mock_node::mock_node;

  DECLARE_PROPERTY(my::own::type_t, prop1);
};
\endcode

The tests performed in this test suite are white-box tests, which is possible thanks to the Qt
public parent child relationships. We only need to make some assumptions on how the widget is
organized internally.
And this coudl add a test similar to:
\code{.cpp}
TEST_F(PropertyWidgetTest, numberProperty)
{
  createWidget();
  auto object = std::make_shared<number_node_mock>("my_own_node_mock", boost::uuids::uuid {});
  auto docu = yoyo::documentation::builder { "Mock own" }
                .property("name", "Name", "", "Name-tooltip", QVariant {})
                .property("prop1", "Property 1", "", "p1-tooltip", QVariant {})
                .build();
  ASSERT_EQ(_widget->children().size(), 5);
  auto content = _widget->children()[4];
  EXPECT_EQ(content->children().size(), 3);

  my::own::type_t prop1;
  EXPECT_CALL(*object, prop1()).WillOnce(Return(prop1));
  object->setName("mockObject1");

  _widget->itemSelected(object, docu);

  EXPECT_EQ(content->children().size(), 5 + 2 * 1); // one additional property
  auto item_count = 1;
  // first row: type
  {
    auto label = dynamic_cast<QLabel*>(content->children()[item_count++]);
    ASSERT_NE(label, nullptr);
    EXPECT_EQ(label->text().toStdString(), "Type");
    label = dynamic_cast<QLabel*>(content->children()[item_count++]);
    ASSERT_NE(label, nullptr);
    EXPECT_EQ(label->text().toStdString(), "number_mock");
  }
  // second row: name
  {
    auto label = dynamic_cast<QLabel*>(content->children()[item_count++]);
    ASSERT_NE(label, nullptr);
    EXPECT_EQ(label->text().toStdString(), std::get<0>(docu->property("name")).toStdString());
    EXPECT_EQ(label->toolTip().toStdString(), std::get<2>(docu->property("name")).toStdString());
    ASSERT_EQ(content->children()[item_count]->metaObject()->className(),
              "yoyo::gui::qstring_input"s);
    ASSERT_EQ(content->children()[item_count]->children().size(), 2);
    auto input = dynamic_cast<QLineEdit*>(content->children()[item_count++]->children()[1]);
    ASSERT_NE(input, nullptr);
    EXPECT_EQ(input->text().toStdString(), "mockObject1");
    EXPECT_EQ(input->parentWidget()->toolTip().toStdString(),
              std::get<2>(docu->property("name")).toStdString());

    object->setName("anOtherName");
    EXPECT_EQ(input->text().toStdString(), "anOtherName");

    input->setText("changedName");
    QTest::keyClick(input, Qt::Key_Return);

    EXPECT_EQ(object->name(), "changedName");
    EXPECT_EQ(input->text().toStdString(), "changedName");
  }
  // prop1: my::own::type_t
  {
    auto label = dynamic_cast<QLabel*>(content->children()[item_count++]);
    ASSERT_NE(label, nullptr);
    EXPECT_EQ(label->text().toStdString(), std::get<0>(docu->property("prop1")).toStdString());
    EXPECT_EQ(label->toolTip().toStdString(), std::get<2>(docu->property("prop1")).toStdString());
    ASSERT_EQ(content->children()[item_count]->metaObject()->className(),
              "my::own::type_input"s);

    // Add checks about the internal composition of the input widget and extract the child or
    // children, which will hold the valuesof my::own::type_t
    ASSERT_EQ(content->children()[item_count]->children().size(), numberOfChildrenInTypeInput);
    auto input = dynamic_cast<WidgetType*>(content->children()[item_count++]->children()[IndexOfInput]);
    ASSERT_NE(input, nullptr);
    EXPECT_EQ(input->text(), expectedDisplay);
    EXPECT_EQ(input->parentWidget()->toolTip().toStdString(),
              std::get<2>(docu->property("prop1")).toStdString());

    // Verifying that the input widget reacts on value changed signal.
    prop1 = {newValue};
    object->prop1Changed(prop1);
    EXPECT_EQ(input->text(), changedDisplay);

    // Verifying that the input widget can set the value for my::own::type_t properly.
    input->setText(nextValue);
    EXPECT_CALL(*object, set_prop1(nextValue));
    QTest::keyClick(input, Qt::Key_Return);
    EXPECT_EQ(input->text(), nextValue);
  }
}
\endcode
\note This is just a simplified example, which doesn't compile. But it is a good starting point for
    the implementation of the actual test.

And, last but not least, since a class deriving from QObject is declared in this source file, the
generated moc file needs to be included (don't worry if the IDE shows it as not found; CMake should
definitely find it). The file-name's stem should be the same as for the test file; extension "moc".
\code{.cpp}
#include "PropertyWidgetOwnTypeTests.moc"
\endcode
*/
